design:faithfulness.overfitting_guards:
- **No business-specific code.** No mechanism names a business, a playbook value from the seven files, a column header or a code. Each one keys on structure:
  - how the reply is shaped (letters, delimiters, commas, typed or not);
  - how many options were picked;
  - the question's kind;
  - whether a statement quotes data;
  - which of the file's own headers or values a sentence names;
  - what was actually written, read back from the file.
- **Plain-English word lists.** The rule-verb list (leave ... out, exclude, combine, same as, divided by, should be ...) and the hedge-word list (often, may, usually ...) are ordinary English, not phrases copied from the owner answers. I checked on the recorded answers that the rule-verb gate does not fire on descriptive answers (ledger class_meaning marks nothing).
- **No thresholds tuned to eval numbers.** There are none, except fixed length caps (1,000 characters for computed notes; told notes are uncapped).
- **Tests don't use the seven eval workbooks.** All proposed tests run on the repo's synthetic fixtures (evals/fixtures: procurement_hotel, ledger_gl, finance_model) or on tiny xlsxwriter books built inside the test.
- **Checks on the seven workbooks were read-only sanity checks.** My replays were read-only prototypes in my own scratch folder, run against copies of the seven workbooks. They showed that each mechanism fires where the diagnoses say it should. They were not used to pick thresholds.
- **The owner settles ambiguity.** Where the tool cannot decide something (which column a leave-out names, how broad it is), it asks the owner with row counts rather than using a heuristic fitted to these files.
- **Lints run over every playbook and every generated question** (option labels, per-option statement vocabulary, gotcha clause provenance, statement length), so they apply equally to the 12 unseen businesses.
